  "full_response": "Git's branching and merging mechanisms are fundamental to its distributed version control model. Here's a detailed look at how Git handles them internally:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Branching in Git**\nBranching in Git is lightweight and efficient because it relies on **pointers** rather than copying files.\n\n#### **How Branches Work Internally**\n- A **branch** in Git is simply a **movable pointer** to a commit.\n- When you create a branch, Git creates a new pointer (e.g., `feature`) that points to the current commit.\n- The default branch is usually `master` or `main`, which is just another pointer.\n\n#### **Key Files & Structures**\n- **`.git/refs/heads/`**: This directory contains files representing local branches. Each file (e.g., `feature`) contains the **SHA-1 hash** of the commit it points to.\n- **HEAD**: A special pointer that indicates the current branch. It is stored in `.git/HEAD` and typically looks like:\n  ```\n  ref: refs/heads/main\n  ```\n  This means `HEAD` points to the `main` branch.\n\n#### **Example: Creating a Branch**\nWhen you run:\n```sh\ngit branch feature\n```\nGit:\n1. Creates a new file `.git/refs/heads/feature`.\n2. Writes the current commit's SHA-1 hash into it.\n3. `HEAD` remains pointing to the current branch (e.g., `main`).\n\nWhen you switch branches (`git checkout feature`), Git:\n1. Updates `HEAD` to point to `feature`.\n2. Updates the working directory to match the commit `feature` points to.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Merging in Git**\nMerging combines changes from different branches. Git uses **three-way merging** (comparing common ancestors) to resolve conflicts.\n\n#### **How Merging Works Internally**\n1. **Find the Merge Base**:\n   - Git identifies the **common ancestor** (the most recent commit shared by both branches).\n   - This is done using the **commit history graph** (DAG - Directed Acyclic Graph).\n\n2. **Three-Way Merge**:\n   - Git compares:\n     - The **merge base** (common ancestor).\n     - The **current branch** (`HEAD`).\n     - The **target branch** (e.g., `feature`).\n   - If changes don’t overlap, Git creates a **merge commit** automatically.\n   - If changes conflict, Git",
